 competitions, datasets, and models to your session\n","# Learn more about kagglehub: https://github.com/Kaggle/kagglehub/blob/main/README.md\n","\n","import kagglehub\n","# kagglehub.dataset_download('<owner>/<dataset-slug>')"]},{"cell_type":"markdown","id":"4a949967","metadata":{"papermill":{"duration":0.003117,"end_time":"2026-10-18T09:28:34.393653+00:00","exception":false,"start_time":"2026-10-18T09:28:34.390536+00:00","status":"completed"},"tags":[]},"source":[" <h1 style=\"font-size: 30px;\">My Approach to Predicting Online Shopping Purchases</h1>\n","\n","<p style=\"font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.8;\">\n","The main goal of this competition is to predict whether a visitor will make a purchase during their visit to an online shopping website. Each row in the dataset represents one visit, and the target column, <code>Revenue</code>, tells us whether that visit resulted in a purchase or not.\n","</p>\n","\n","<p style=\"font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.8;\">\n","The competition uses <code>ROC-AUC</code> as its evaluation metric. In simple words, it checks how well our model can place visitors who are likely to make a purchase above those who are less likely to buy something. So, instead of focusing only on the final predicted values, I focused on improving the order in which the model ranks the visitors.\n","</p>\n","\n","<hr>\n","\n","<h2 style=\"font-size: 24px;\">1. Understanding the Data (EDA)</h2>\n","\n","<p style=\"font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.8;\">\n","Before building any model, I first wanted to understand the dataset properly. I checked the number of rows and columns in both the training and test datasets. I also looked at the first few rows to understand what information was available and how each column could help predict a purchase.\n","</p>\n","\n","<p style=\"font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.8;\">\n","I used <code>info()</code> and <code>describe()</code> to check the data types, missing values, and basic statistics of the numerical columns. This gave me a better idea of the data I was working with and helped me decide what needed further checking.\n","</p>\n","\n","<p style=\"font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.8;\">\n","After that, I plotted histograms for important columns such as <code>PageValues</code>, <code>BounceRates</code>, <code>ExitRates</code>, page durations, and page counts. I noticed that many of these columns had a similar pattern. Most visitors had small or zero values, while a few visitors had much larger values.\n","</p>\n","\n","<p style=\"font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.8;\">\n","I also created a correlation heatmap to understand how different columns were related to each other and to the target column, <code>Revenue</code>. One thing that caught my attention was <code>PageValues</code>, as it showed a strong relationship with purchasing behaviour. This made it an important feature to study while building the models.\n","</p>\n","\n","<p style=\"font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.8;\">\n","Finally, I checked the number of unique values in each column. This helped me identify columns such as <code>Browser</code>, <code>Region</code>, <code>TrafficType</code>, and <code>OperatingSystems</code>. Although these columns contain numbers, the numbers represent different categories rather than actual quantities. For example, Browser 8 is not necessarily greater or better than Browser 2.\n","</p>\n","\n","<hr>\n","\n","<h2 style=\"font-size: 24px;\">2. Cleaning the Data and Creating New Features</h2>\n","\n","<p style=\"font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.8;\">\n","Once I had a better understanding of the data, I moved on to cleaning it and creating some additional features. My aim was to give the models more useful information about how visitors behaved during their shopping sessions.\n","</p>\n","\n","<p style=\"font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.8;\">\n","I first checked the duration-related columns. Since negative time values do not make sense in this situation, I treated negative durations as missing values rather than allowing them to be used as normal time measurements.\n","</p>\n","\n","<p style=\"font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.8;\">\n","I then created new features by combining information that was already present in the dataset. For example, I worked on features related to the total number of pages visited, the time spent during a session, and the average time spent per page. These features could help the model understand whether a visitor was simply browsing or spending more time exploring the website.\n","</p>\n","\n","<p style=\"font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.8;\">\n","I also created features using page counts and their proportions. The idea was to understand how much of a visitor's activity was related to different types of pages. Along with this, I used the difference between <code>ExitRates</code> and <code>BounceRates</code> to provide the model with another way to study visitor behaviour.\n","</p>\n","\n","<p style=\"font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.8;\">\n","Another feature I created was a simple indicator to show whether a session had any non-zero <code>PageValues</code>. I also experimented with combining <code>PageValues</code> with page counts and session duration. My intention was to help the model identify useful patterns that might not be as clear when these values are considered separately.\n","</p>\n","\n","<p style=\"font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.8;\">\n","The <code>Month</code> column was stored as text, so I converted the month names into numbers from 1 to 12. This made the natural order of the months clear while keeping the information about the time of the year.\n","</p>\n","\n","<p style=\"font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.8;\">\n","I also handled the category columns differently depending on the model. For XGBoost and LightGBM, I used one-hot encoding to represent the categories. For CatBoost, I provided the categorical columns directly because it can handle categorical data on its own.\n","</p>\n","\n","<p style=\"font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.8;\">\n","Overall, the purpose of this step was not just to add more columns. I wanted to create features that could help the models understand visitor activity more clearly and make better predictions.\n","</p>\n","\n","<hr>\n","\n","<h2 style=\"font-size: 24px;\">3. Building and Improving the Models</h2>\n","\n","<p style=\"font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.8;\">\n","After preparing the data, I started experimenting with different machine learning models. Instead of depending on just one model, I decided to work with XGBoost, CatBoost, and LightGBM. All three are useful for working with table-based data, but each of them learns patterns in a slightly different way.\n","</p>\n","\n","<h3 style=\"font-size: 20px;\">XGBoost</h3>\n","\n","<p style=\"font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.8;\">\n","I started with XGBoost because it is a strong model for this type of problem. During my first experiments, I tried very deep trees, with the maximum depth going up to 13. However, I realised that using very deep trees on a dataset with around 12,000 rows could make the model learn too much from the training data instead of learning patterns that would work well on new visitors.\n","</p>\n","\n","<p style=\"font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.8;\">\n","To improve this, I moved towards shallower trees and adjusted parameters such as <code>gamma</code>, <code>reg_lambda</code>, <code>reg_alpha</code>, and <code>min_child_weight</code>. These settings help control how complex the model becomes and reduce the chances of overfitting.\n","</p>\n","\n","<p style=\"font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.8;\">\n","I also used randomized hyperparameter search instead of trying every possible combination of settings. This allowed me to test different configurations more efficiently and focus on finding a model that performed well on unseen data.\n","</p>\n","\n","<h3 style=\"font-size: 20px;\">CatBoost</h3>\n","\n","<p style=\"font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.8;\">\n","Next, I experimented with CatBoost. One of its main advantages is that it can work directly with categorical columns. This was useful because the dataset contains features such as <code>Browser</code>, <code>Region</code>, and <code>TrafficType</code>.\n","</p>\n","\n","<p style=\"font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.8;\">\n","Instead of manually converting these categories into multiple columns, I provided them to CatBoost as categorical features. I then used randomized search to try different settings and find a suitable configuration for the dataset.\n","</p>\n","\n","<h3 style=\"font-size: 20px;\">LightGBM</h3>\n","\n","<p style=\"font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.8;\">\n","Finally, I added LightGBM to the experiment. I tried relatively shallow trees and used early stopping during training. Early stopping helps prevent unnecessary training by checking the model's performance on validation data and stopping when further training no longer provides an improvement.\n","</p>\n","\n","<p style=\"font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.8;\">\n","The main idea behind using these three models was to learn from different approaches rather than depending entirely on one model. I wanted each model to perform well individually while also checking whether their predictions could work better when combined.\n","</p>\n","\n","<hr>\n","\n","<h2 style=\"font-size: 24px;\">4. Cross-Validation and Model Evaluation</h2>\n","\n","<p style=\"font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.8;\">\n","One important part of this competition was checking whether the models could perform well on data they had not seen before. A model may perform very well on its training data but still make poor predictions on new data. To avoid depending only on training performance, I used 5-fold stratified cross-validation.\n","</p>\n","\n","<p style=\"font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.8;\">\n","In this method, the training dataset is divided into five parts. The model trains on four parts and is tested on the remaining part. This process is repeated five times, with each part being used for validation once.\n","</p>\n","\n","<p style=\"font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.8;\">\n","I used stratified folds so that the proportion of visitors who made a purchase and those who did not remained approximately similar across the different parts. This is useful because purchase prediction datasets often contain fewer purchasing sessions than non-purchasing sessions.\n","</p>\n","\n","<p style=\"font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.8;\">\n","During this process, I generated out-of-fold (OOF) predictions. These are predictions made for training rows by models that did not use those particular rows for training. This gave me a more reliable way to check how well the models might perform on unseen data.\n","</p>\n","\n","<p style=\"font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.8;\">\n","I also repeated the experiments using three different random seeds and averaged the predictions. Since changing the random seed can change how the data is divided into folds, using multiple seeds helped reduce the effect of getting an unusually good or bad split.\n","</p>\n","\n","<p style=\"font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.8;\">\n","For the test dataset, I averaged predictions from the models trained across the different folds. This helped me make use of the models trained on different portions of the training data rather than relying on a single model.\n","</p>\n","\n","<hr>\n","\n","<h2 style=\"font-size: 24px;\">5. Combining the Models Using Rank Averaging</h2>\n","\n","<p style=\"font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.8;\">\n","After experimenting with the individual models, I combined XGBoost, CatBoost, and LightGBM into one final ensemble. The reason for doing this was simple: different models may find different patterns in the same dataset, and they may make different mistakes.\n","</p>\n","\n","<p style=\"font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.8;\">\n","Instead of directly averaging their predicted probabilities, I used rank averaging. In this approach, the predictions from each model are converted into ranks, and the ranks are then averaged to create the final predictions.\n","</p>\n","\n","<p style=\"font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.8;\">\n","For example, one model might give a visitor a probability of 0.80, while another might give the same visitor 0.65. These values do not necessarily mean that the models have the same level of confidence. However, both models may place that visitor among the people most likely to make a purchase.\n","</p>\n","\n","<p style=\"font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.8;\">\n","Rank averaging focuses more on the position of each visitor in the predictions instead of the exact probability produced by each model. This makes it a useful approach for a competition evaluated using ROC-AUC, where the order of the predictions is especially important.\n","</p>\n","\n","<p style=\"font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.8;\">\n","By combining the ranks from the three models, I aimed to create predictions that were less dependent on the strengths and weaknesses of any single model. However, blending does not always improve the score, so the final decision should still be based on proper validation.\n","</p>\n","\n","<hr>\n","\n","<h2 style=\"font-size: 24px;\">6. Why I Chose This Approach</h2>\n","\n","<p style=\"font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.8;\">\n","Throughout the competition, I tried to focus on making steady improvements rather than simply making the models more complicated.\n","</p>\n","\n","<p style=\"font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.8;\">\n","First, I paid attention to overfitting. Since the dataset contains around 12,000 rows, very deep trees may learn details that are specific to the training data. Using shallower trees and suitable regularisation helped me control the complexity of the models.\n","</p>\n","\n","<p style=\"font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.8;\">\n","Second, I focused on creating useful features. Information such as the number of pages visited, time spent on the website, and page values can help describe visitor behaviour. By combining some of these features, I tried to give the models additional information that could help them separate likely buyers from other visitors.\n","</p>\n","\n","<p style=\"font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.8;\">\n","Third, I used cross-validation and multiple random seeds to check whether the results were consistent. I did not want to choose a model simply because it performed well on one particular split of the data.\n","</p>\n","\n","<p style=\"font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.8;\">\n","Finally, I used an ensemble of three different models because each model may capture patterns that the others miss. Rank averaging allowed me to combine their predictions in a way that matched the main goal of the competition.\n","</p>\n","\n","<p style=\"font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.8;\">\n","I also kept in mind that the public leaderboard only shows performance on a particular part of the test data. A small improvement in the public score does not always mean that the model will perform better on the full hidden test set. For this reason, I used cross-validation as my main guide when comparing different approaches.\n","</p>\n","\n","<hr>\n","\n","<h2 style=\"font-size: 24px;\">7. Final Conclusion</h2>\n","\n","<p style=\"font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.8;\">\n","This competition helped me understand that building a good machine learning model involves much more than choosing an algorithm and training it. Understanding the data, cleaning incorrect values, creating useful features, selecting suitable parameters, and testing the model properly are all important parts of the process.\n","</p>\n","\n","<p style=\"font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.8;\">\n","I began by exploring the dataset and studying visitor behaviour. I then created additional features, experimented with XGBoost, CatBoost, and LightGBM, and used cross-validation to compare their performance. After that, I combined the models using rank averaging to make the final predictions.\n","</p>\n","\n","<p style=\"font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.8;\">\n","My overall goal was to build a model that could rank visitors according to how likely they were to make a purchase, while avoiding unnecessary complexity and overfitting.\n","</p>\n","\n","<p style=\"font-size: 18px; line-height: 1.8;\">\n","In the end, I focused on a simple principle: <span style=\"font-size: 18px;\"><b>understand the data, learn from experiments, validate carefully, and combine models only when they provide useful improvements.</b></span>\n","</p>"]},{"cell_type":"markdown","id":"1aa12588","metadata":{"editable":false,"papermill":{"duration":0.002742,"end_time":"2026-10-18T09:28:34.399632+00:00","exception":false,"start_time":"2026-10-18T09:28:34.39689+00:00","status":"completed"},"tags":[]},"source":["<span style=\"font-size:35px\">Data Loading and Shape Analysis"]},{"cell_type":"code","execution_count":2,"id":"4f76f628","metadata":{"editable":false,"execution":{"iopub.execute_input":"2026-10-18T09:28:34.406909Z","iopub.status.busy":"2026-10-18T09:28:34.406315Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-10-18T09:28:34.61669Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-10-18T09:28:34.615665Z"},"papermill":{"duration":0.216543,"end_time":"2026-10-18T09:28:34.618892+00:00","exception":false,"start_time":"2026-10-18T09:28:34.402349+00:00","status":"completed"},"tags":[]},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","output_type":"stream","text":["Train Shape: (19568, 19)\n","Test Shape: (10431, 18)\n"]},{"data":{"text/html":["<div>\n","<style scoped>\n","    .dataframe tbody tr th:only-of-type {\n","        vertical-align: middle;\n","    }\n","\n","    .dataframe tbody tr th {\n","        vertical-align: top;\n","    }\n","\n","    .dataframe thead th {\n","        text-align: right;\n","    }\n","</style>\n","<table border=\"1\" class=\"dataframe\">\n","  <thead>\n","    <tr style=\"text-align: right;\">\n","      <th></th>\n","      <th>id</th>\n","      <th>Administrative</th>\n","      <th>Administrative_Duration</th>\n","      <th>Informa